{"surah": 75, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "لَآ أُقْسِمُ بِيَوْمِ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ ", "translation": "I swear by the Day of Resurrection", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَآ", "translit": "ٓāَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أُقْسِمُ", "translit": "ُmِsْqُaʾ", "root": "قسم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِيَوْمِ", "translit": "ِmْwَyِb", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmَٰyِqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "وَلَآ أُقْسِمُ بِٱلنَّفْسِ ٱللَّوَّامَةِ ", "translation": "And I swear by the reproaching soul[1] [to the certainty of resurrection].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَآ", "translit": "ٓāَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أُقْسِمُ", "translit": "ُmِsْqُaʾ", "root": "قسم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِٱلنَّفْسِ", "translit": "ِsْfَّnlٱِb", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّوَّامَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmāَّwَّllٱ", "root": "لوم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "أَيَحْسَبُ ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ أَلَّن نَّجْمَعَ عِظَامَهُۥ ", "translation": "Does man think that We will not assemble his bones?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَيَحْسَبُ", "translit": "ُbَsْḥَyَaʾ", "root": "حسب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَلَّن", "translit": "nَّlَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "نَّجْمَعَ", "translit": "َʿعَmْjgَّn", "root": "جمع"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عِظَامَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَmāَẓِʿع", "root": "عظم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "بَلَىٰ قَٰدِرِينَ عَلَىٰٓ أَن نُّسَوِّىَ بَنَانَهُۥ ", "translation": "Yes. [We are] Able [even] to proportion his fingertips.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "بَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَٰدِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِdَٰq", "root": "قدر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "نُّسَوِّىَ", "translit": "َàِّwَsُّn", "root": "سوي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بَنَانَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَnāَnَb", "root": "بنن"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "بَلْ يُرِيدُ ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ لِيَفْجُرَ أَمَامَهُۥ ", "translation": "But man desires to continue in sin.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يُرِيدُ", "translit": "ُdyِrُy", "root": "رود"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِيَفْجُرَ", "translit": "َrُjgْfَyِl", "root": "فجر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَمَامَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَmāَmَaʾ", "root": "امم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "يَسْـَٔلُ أَيَّانَ يَوْمُ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ ", "translation": "He asks, \"When is the Day of Resurrection?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَسْـَٔلُ", "translit": "ُlَٔـْsَy", "root": "سال"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَيَّانَ", "translit": "َnāَّyَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَوْمُ", "translit": "ُmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَmَٰyِqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "فَإِذَا بَرِقَ ٱلْبَصَرُ ", "translation": "So when vision is dazzled", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بَرِقَ", "translit": "َqِrَb", "root": "برق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْبَصَرُ", "translit": "ُrَṣَbْlٱ", "root": "بصر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "وَخَسَفَ ٱلْقَمَرُ ", "translation": "And the moon darkens", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَخَسَفَ", "translit": "َfَsَhkَw", "root": "خسف"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْقَمَرُ", "translit": "ُrَmَqْlٱ", "root": "قمر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "وَجُمِعَ ٱلشَّمْسُ وَٱلْقَمَرُ ", "translation": "And the sun and the moon are joined,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَجُمِعَ", "translit": "َʿعِmُjgَw", "root": "جمع"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلشَّمْسُ", "translit": "ُsْmَّhslٱ", "root": "شمس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَٱلْقَمَرُ", "translit": "ُrَmَqْlٱَw", "root": "قمر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "يَقُولُ ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ يَوْمَئِذٍ أَيْنَ ٱلْمَفَرُّ ", "translation": "Man will say on that Day, \"Where is the [place of] escape?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَقُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُqَy", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَوْمَئِذٍ", "translit": "ٍhdِyʾَmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَيْنَ", "translit": "َnْyَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْمَفَرُّ", "translit": "ُّrَfَmْlٱ", "root": "فرر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "كَلَّا لَا وَزَرَ ", "translation": "No! There is no refuge.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كَلَّا", "translit": "āَّlَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَزَرَ", "translit": "َrَzَw", "root": "وزر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "إِلَىٰ رَبِّكَ يَوْمَئِذٍ ٱلْمُسْتَقَرُّ ", "translation": "To your Lord, that Day, is the [place of] permanence.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَوْمَئِذٍ", "translit": "ٍhdِyʾَmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْمُسْتَقَرُّ", "translit": "ُّrَqَtْsُmْlٱ", "root": "قرر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 13, "text": "يُنَبَّؤُا۟ ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ يَوْمَئِذٍۭ بِمَا قَدَّمَ وَأَخَّرَ ", "translation": "Man will be informed that Day of what he sent ahead[1] and kept back.[2]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يُنَبَّؤُا۟", "translit": "۟āُwʾَّbَnُy", "root": "نبا"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَوْمَئِذٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭhdِyʾَmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَدَّمَ", "translit": "َmَّdَq", "root": "قدم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَأَخَّرَ", "translit": "َrَّhkَaʾَw", "root": "اخر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 14, "text": "بَلِ ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ عَلَىٰ نَفْسِهِۦ بَصِيرَةٌ ", "translation": "Rather, man, against himself, will be a witness,[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "بَلِ", "translit": "ِlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "نَفْسِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِsْfَn", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَصِيرَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَryِṣَb", "root": "بصر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 15, "text": "وَلَوْ أَلْقَىٰ مَعَاذِيرَهُۥ ", "translation": "Even if he presents his excuses.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَوْ", "translit": "ْwَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَلْقَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَqْlَaʾ", "root": "لقي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَعَاذِيرَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَryِhdāَʿعَm", "root": "عذر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 16, "text": "لَا تُحَرِّكْ بِهِۦ لِسَانَكَ لِتَعْجَلَ بِهِۦٓ ", "translation": "Move not your tongue with it, [O Muḥammad], to hasten with it [i.e., recitation of the Qur’ān].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تُحَرِّكْ", "translit": "ْkِّrَḥُt", "root": "حرك"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِسَانَكَ", "translit": "َkَnāَsِl", "root": "لسن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لِتَعْجَلَ", "translit": "َlَjgْʿعَtِl", "root": "عجل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِهِۦٓ", "translit": "ٓۦِhِb", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 17, "text": "إِنَّ عَلَيْنَا جَمْعَهُۥ وَقُرْءَانَهُۥ ", "translation": "Indeed, upon Us is its collection [in your heart] and [to make possible] its recitation.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "جَمْعَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَʿعْmَjg", "root": "جمع"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَقُرْءَانَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَnāَءْrُqَw", "root": "قرا"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 18, "text": "فَإِذَا قَرَأْنَٰهُ فَٱتَّبِعْ قُرْءَانَهُۥ ", "translation": "So when We have recited it [through Gabriel], then follow its recitation.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَإِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَرَأْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْaʾَrَq", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَٱتَّبِعْ", "translit": "ْʿعِbَّtٱَf", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "قُرْءَانَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَnāَءْrُq", "root": "قرا"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 19, "text": "ثُمَّ إِنَّ عَلَيْنَا بَيَانَهُۥ ", "translation": "Then upon Us is its clarification [to you].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بَيَانَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَnāَyَb", "root": "بين"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 20, "text": "كَلَّا بَلْ تُحِبُّونَ ٱلْعَاجِلَةَ ", "translation": "No! But you [i.e., mankind] love the immediate", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كَلَّا", "translit": "āَّlَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تُحِبُّونَ", "translit": "َnwُّbِḥُt", "root": "حبب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْعَاجِلَةَ", "translit": "َẗَlِjgāَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عجل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 21, "text": "وَتَذَرُونَ ٱلْءَاخِرَةَ ", "translation": "And leave [i.e., neglect] the Hereafter.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَتَذَرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrَhdَtَw", "root": "وذر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْءَاخِرَةَ", "translit": "َẗَrِhkāَءْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 22, "text": "وُجُوهٌ يَوْمَئِذٍ نَّاضِرَةٌ ", "translation": "[Some] faces, that Day, will be radiant,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وُجُوهٌ", "translit": "ٌhwُjgُw", "root": "وجه"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَوْمَئِذٍ", "translit": "ٍhdِyʾَmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نَّاضِرَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَrِḍāَّn", "root": "نضر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 23, "text": "إِلَىٰ رَبِّهَا نَاظِرَةٌ ", "translation": "Looking at their Lord.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَبِّهَا", "translit": "āَhِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نَاظِرَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَrِẓāَn", "root": "نظر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 24, "text": "وَوُجُوهٌ يَوْمَئِذٍۭ بَاسِرَةٌ ", "translation": "And [some] faces, that Day, will be contorted,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَوُجُوهٌ", "translit": "ٌhwُjgُwَw", "root": "وجه"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَوْمَئِذٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭhdِyʾَmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَاسِرَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَrِsāَb", "root": "بسر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 25, "text": "تَظُنُّ أَن يُفْعَلَ بِهَا فَاقِرَةٌ ", "translation": "Expecting that there will be done to them [something] backbreaking.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "تَظُنُّ", "translit": "ُّnُẓَt", "root": "ظنن"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُفْعَلَ", "translit": "َlَʿعْfُy", "root": "فعل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِهَا", "translit": "āَhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَاقِرَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَrِqāَf", "root": "فقر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 26, "text": "كَلَّآ إِذَا بَلَغَتِ ٱلتَّرَاقِىَ ", "translation": "No! When it [i.e., the soul] has reached the collar bones[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كَلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَلَغَتِ", "translit": "ِtَغَlَb", "root": "بلغ"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلتَّرَاقِىَ", "translit": "َàِqāَrَّtlٱ", "root": "ترق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 27, "text": "وَقِيلَ مَنْۜ رَاقٍ ", "translation": "And it is said, \"Who will cure [him]?\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقِيلَ", "translit": "َlyِqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَنْ", "translit": "ْnَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَاقٍ", "translit": "ٍqāَr", "root": "رقي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 28, "text": "وَظَنَّ أَنَّهُ ٱلْفِرَاقُ ", "translation": "And he [i.e., the dying one] is certain that it is the [time of] separation", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَظَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَẓَw", "root": "ظنن"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْفِرَاقُ", "translit": "ُqāَrِfْlٱ", "root": "فرق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 29, "text": "وَٱلْتَفَّتِ ٱلسَّاقُ بِٱلسَّاقِ ", "translation": "And the leg is wound about the leg,[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلْتَفَّتِ", "translit": "ِtَّfَtْlٱَw", "root": "لفف"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلسَّاقُ", "translit": "ُqāَّslٱ", "root": "سوق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِٱلسَّاقِ", "translit": "ِqāَّslٱِb", "root": "سوق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 30, "text": "إِلَىٰ رَبِّكَ يَوْمَئِذٍ ٱلْمَسَاقُ ", "translation": "To your Lord, that Day, will be the procession.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَوْمَئِذٍ", "translit": "ٍhdِyʾَmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْمَسَاقُ", "translit": "ُqāَsَmْlٱ", "root": "سوق"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 31, "text": "فَلَا صَدَّقَ وَلَا صَلَّىٰ ", "translation": "And he [i.e., the disbeliever] had not believed, nor had he prayed.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "صَدَّقَ", "translit": "َqَّdَṣ", "root": "صدق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "صَلَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّlَṣ", "root": "صلو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 32, "text": "وَلَٰكِن كَذَّبَ وَتَوَلَّىٰ ", "translation": "But [instead], he denied and turned away.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَٰكِن", "translit": "nِkَٰlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَذَّبَ", "translit": "َbَّhdَk", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَتَوَلَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّlَwَtَw", "root": "ولي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 33, "text": "ثُمَّ ذَهَبَ إِلَىٰٓ أَهْلِهِۦ يَتَمَطَّىٰٓ ", "translation": "And then he went to his people, swaggering [in pride].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ذَهَبَ", "translit": "َbَhَhd", "root": "ذهب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَهْلِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِlْhَaʾ", "root": "اهل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَتَمَطَّىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَّṭَmَtَy", "root": "مطو"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 34, "text": "أَوْلَىٰ لَكَ فَأَوْلَىٰ ", "translation": "Woe to you, and woe!", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَوْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْwَaʾ", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَكَ", "translit": "َkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَأَوْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْwَaʾَf", "root": "ولي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 35, "text": "ثُمَّ أَوْلَىٰ لَكَ فَأَوْلَىٰٓ ", "translation": "Then woe to you, and woe!", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَوْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْwَaʾ", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَكَ", "translit": "َkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَأَوْلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlْwَaʾَf", "root": "ولي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 36, "text": "أَيَحْسَبُ ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ أَن يُتْرَكَ سُدًى ", "translation": "Does man think that he will be left neglected?[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَيَحْسَبُ", "translit": "ُbَsْḥَyَaʾ", "root": "حسب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يُتْرَكَ", "translit": "َkَrْtُy", "root": "ترك"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "سُدًى", "translit": "àًdُs", "root": "سدي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 37, "text": "أَلَمْ يَكُ نُطْفَةً مِّن مَّنِىٍّ يُمْنَىٰ ", "translation": "Had he not been a sperm from semen emitted?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَكُ", "translit": "ُkَy", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نُطْفَةً", "translit": "ًẗَfْṭُn", "root": "نطف"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَّنِىٍّ", "translit": "ٍّàِnَّm", "root": "مني"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يُمْنَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَnْmُy", "root": "مني"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 38, "text": "ثُمَّ كَانَ عَلَقَةً فَخَلَقَ فَسَوَّىٰ ", "translation": "Then he was a clinging clot, and [Allāh] created [his form] and proportioned [him]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَقَةً", "translit": "ًẗَqَlَʿع", "root": "علق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَخَلَقَ", "translit": "َqَlَhkَf", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَسَوَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّwَsَf", "root": "سوي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 39, "text": "فَجَعَلَ مِنْهُ ٱلزَّوْجَيْنِ ٱلذَّكَرَ وَٱلْأُنثَىٰٓ ", "translation": "And made of him two mates, the male and the female.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَجَعَلَ", "translit": "َlَʿعَjgَf", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِنْهُ", "translit": "ُhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلزَّوْجَيْنِ", "translit": "ِnْyَjgْwَّzlٱ", "root": "زوج"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلذَّكَرَ", "translit": "َrَkَّhdlٱ", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱلْأُنثَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَhtnُaʾْlٱَw", "root": "انث"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 40, "text": "أَلَيْسَ ذَٰلِكَ بِقَٰدِرٍ عَلَىٰٓ أَن يُحْۦِىَ ٱلْمَوْتَىٰ ", "translation": "Is not that [Creator] Able to give life to the dead?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَلَيْسَ", "translit": "َsْyَlَaʾ", "root": "ليس"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِقَٰدِرٍ", "translit": "ٍrِdَٰqِb", "root": "قدر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَلَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يُحْۦِىَ", "translit": "َàِۦْḥُy", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْمَوْتَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَtْwَmْlٱ", "root": "موت"}], "hasIhya": true}], "tafsir": {"2": [{"arabic": "قال الله تعالى {ولا أقسم بالنفس اللوامة} وإن تركت الاعتراض وأذعنت وأطاعت لمقتضى الشهوات ودواعي الشيطان سميت النفس الأمارة بالسوء قال الله تعالى إخبارا عن يوسف عليه السلام أو امرأة العزيز {وما أبرئ نفسي إن النفس لأمارة بالسوء} وقد يجوز أن يقال المراد بالأمارة بالسوء هي النفس بالمعنى الأول فإذن النفس بالمعنى الأول مذمومة غاية الذم وبالمعنى الثاني محمودة لأنها نفس الإنسان أي ذاته وحقيقته العالمة بالله تعالى وسائر المعلومات اللفظ الرابع العقل وهو أيضا مشترك لمعان مختلفة ذكرناها في كتا", "english": "The second meaning [of nafs] is the subtle reality (al-laṭīfah) which we have mentioned — that which is truly the human being. It is the soul of man and his very essence (dhāt), but it is described by different attributes according to the variation of its states. When it settles under the command [of God] and the agitation caused by the opposition of the passions departs from it, it is called the tranquil soul (al-nafs al-muṭma'innah). God Most High said concerning its like: {«يَا أَيَّتُهَا النَّفْسُ الْمُطْمَئِنَّةُ ارْجِعِي إِلَىٰ رَبِّكِ رَاضِيَةً مَّرْضِيَّةً»} — \"O tranquil soul, return to your Lord, well-pleased and well-pleasing.\" But the soul in the first meaning cannot be conceived to return to God Most High, for it is banished far from God and is of the party of Satan.", "book_title": "Monitoring and Accounting", "section_title_ar": "Section 574", "section_title_en": "The Comprehensive Principle of Blameworthy Traits and the True Nature of the Soul, Spirit, and Intellect", "section_index": 574, "anchors": "- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, breath; that which animates and individuates a being\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-nafs denotes the rūḥ (spirit) in certain Quranic usages, but also the very dhāt (essence/self) of a thing—as in {«وَيُحَذِّرُكُمُ اللَّهُ نَفْسَهُ»} where \"His Self\" means His Essence, not a separate entity\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage denotes the blood of life (\"nafs sā'ilah\" — flowing blood); metaphorical (majāz) usage extends to the inner self, the eye of envy, and the puerperal woman\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn over, to flip, to transform from one state to another\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Taqlīb is the constant turning and disposition of a thing from one state to another; the heart is so named because of its perpetual fluctuation of states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: turning a thing over, inverting it; Metaphorical: the heart as the seat of understanding and discernment—\"قلب المعلم الصبيان\" (the teacher turns the boys [toward their homes]) extends to directing the intellect\n- Root: علم (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Knowledge, perception, discernment; the mark or sign by which something is known\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): ʿIlm is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality (idrāk al-shay' bi-ḥaqīqatihi)—two kinds: (1) apprehension of the essence of a thing, (2) judgment upon a thing affirming or denying something of it\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to know, to be aware of; also used for the landmarks (aʿlām) and traces by which one is guided on the path\n- Root: انس ('-n-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To perceive, to become familiar with, to find comfort in\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The ins (human) is so called for his sociability and perceptive nature, contrasted with the jinn (from tajannun — hiddenness)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to find comfort and familiarity with someone; Metaphorical: \"ibn urns\" — the intimate companion, the special confidant\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the permissibility of interposing elements between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase, relevant here for parsing the grammatical relationships in complex attributive constructions distinguishing the various referents of the terms under discussion.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monitoring and Accounting'", "badge": "Insight"}], "13": [{"arabic": "أيضا ما أخروه من آثار أعمالهم كما نكتب ما قدموه وفي مثله قوله تعالى {ينبأ الإنسان يومئذ بما قدم وأخر} وإنما أخر آثار أعماله من سنة سيئة عمل بها غيره وليعلم أن في الزيف خمسة أمور الأول أنه إذا رد عليه شيء منه فينبغي أن يطرحه في بئر بحيث لا تمتد إليه اليد وإياه أن يروجه في بيع آخر وإن أفسده بحيث لا يمكن التعامل به جاز الثاني أنه يجب على التاجر تعلم النقد لا ليستقصي لنفسه ولكن لئلا يسلم إلى مسلم زيفا وهو لا يدري فيكون آثما بتقصيره في تعلم ذلك العلم فكل علم عمل به يتم نصح المسلمين فيجب تحصيله ولمثل هذا كان السلف يتعلمون علامات النقد نظرا لدينهم", "english": "Know that the circulation of counterfeit coinage (*al-zuyūf*) during the course of payment is an injustice (*ẓulm*), for the one who transacts with it is harmed thereby — if he does not recognize it [as counterfeit], he is injured; and if he does recognize it, he will circulate it to another. And so likewise with the third and the fourth [recipient]. It continues to pass from hand to hand, the harm becomes general, the corruption spreads widely, and the burden (*wizr*) of all of it and its evil consequence reverts upon him [the originator] — for he it is who opened this door. The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, said: {«مَنْ سَنَّ سُنَّةً سَيِّئَةً فَعُمِلَ بِهَا مِنْ بَعْدِهِ كَانَ عَلَيْهِ وِزْرُهَا وَمِثْلُ وِزْرِ مَنْ عَمِلَ بِهَا لَا يَنْقُصُ مِنْ أَوْزَارِهِمْ شَيْئًا»} (\"Whoever institutes an evil practice and it is acted upon after him, upon him is its burden and the like of the burden of those who act upon it, without diminishing anything from their burdens.\")", "book_title": "Etiquette of Earning", "section_title_ar": "Section 324", "section_title_en": "Section 324 — On the Circulation of Counterfeit Coinage and Its Grave Consequences", "section_index": 324, "anchors": "- **Root: زيف (z-y-f)**\n  * *Lisan / Ayn*: Core meaning of falsification, adulteration, and counterfeiting — that which is false in its substance, not merely in its claim.\n  * *Al-Raghib (Mufradat)*: The theological dimension concerns the *spreading of corruption* (ifsād) through deceptive transactions — a form of *ẓulm* (injustice) that multiplies as the counterfeit passes from hand to hand.\n  * *Al-Zamakhshari (Asas)*: Literal (*ḥaqīqah*): false coinage. Metaphorical (*majāz*): the *sunnah sayyiʾah* (evil precedent) — the one who originates it bears its burden and the burden of those who follow it.\n\n- **Root: وزر (w-z-r)**\n  * *Lisan / Ayn*: The heavy burden (*al-wizr*), the load one carries; also the mountain refuge. The *wāzir* is the bearer of the burden.\n  * *Al-Raghib (Mufradat)*: Used for sin (*ithm*) as a heavy burden upon the soul. The one who originates an evil practice carries his own burden and the burdens of those who follow him — as in {«وَلَيَحْمِلُنَّ أَثْقَالَهُمْ وَأَثْقَالًا مَّعَ أَثْقَالِهِمْ»} (Al-ʿAnkabūt: 13).\n  * *Al-Zamakhshari (Asas)*: Literal: carrying a physical load. Metaphorical: bearing the weight of sin — \"he is *mawzūr*, not *maʾjūr*\" (burdened with sin, not rewarded).\n\n- **Root: سنن (s-n-n)**\n  * *Lisan / Ayn*: The path or way (*al-ṭarīqah*); to institute a practice or precedent.\n  * *Al-Raghib (Mufradat)*: *Sunnah* as the established pattern — whether good or evil. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Whoever institutes a good sunnah...\" and \"Whoever institutes an evil sunnah...\"\n  * *Al-Zamakhshari (Asas)*: Literal: the beaten path. Metaphorical: the moral precedent that others follow — its originator is accountable for all who walk it after him.\n\n- **Sibawayh Rule**: *Bāb al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā* (Restriction and Focused Predication) — used in the phrase \"إنما يتخلص من إثم الضرر الذي يخص معامله فقط\" to restrict the scope of absolution to the specific harm done to one's immediate transactor, not the general harm propagated through subsequent circulation.\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Etiquette of Earning'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "18": [{"arabic": "تعالى فإذا قرأناه فاتبع قرآنه قيل في التفسير معناه إذا قرأه عليك جبريل وقال تعالى قاتلوهم يعذبهم الله بأيديكم فأضاف القتل إليهم والتعذيب إلى نفسه والتعذيب هو عين", "english": "Thus should you understand the issuance of determined things (al-maqdūrāt) from eternal power — even though the power is eternal (qadīmah) and the objects of power are originated (ḥādithah). And this is a knocking upon another door of another world among the worlds of unveilings (mukāshafāt). So let us leave all of that, for our purpose is only to alert to the path of unification (tawḥīd) in the act — for the agent in truth (al-fāʿil bi-al-ḥaqīqah) is One. He is the feared and the hoped, and upon Him is reliance and trust. And we have not been able to mention from the seas of tawḥīd save a drop from the ocean of the third station among the stations of tawḥīd. And exhausting that within the lifetime of Noah is impossible — like exhausting the water of the sea by taking droplets from it. And all of that is subsumed under the saying: \"There is no god but God\" (lā ilāha illā Allāh). How light is its burden upon the tongue, how easy is the belief in the meaning of its utterance upon the heart, and how precious is its reality and its kernel (lubbuhu) among the scholars firmly rooted in knowledge — so how [much more so] with others?", "book_title": "Monotheism and Reliance", "section_title_ar": "Section 1114", "section_title_en": "The Oneness of the Actor (Tawḥīd al-Fiʿl) — Reconciling Divine and Human Agency", "section_index": 1114, "anchors": "- Root: قدر (q-d-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes measure, decree, and capacity; al-Farāhīdī anchors it in the physical act of measuring or sizing, extended to divine determining.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-qudrah\" when ascribed to God negates incapacity (ʿajz) absolutely; when ascribed to man, it names a state (hayʾah) enabling a specific act — never absolute power, but restricted capacity over particular objects.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"He is qādir, possessing power.\" Metaphorical (majāz): \"faras baʿīd al-qadr\" (a far-striding horse) — power's \"reach\" extended figuratively; here, divine power's \"presence\" with its objects is literal for God, metaphorical for creatures.\n- Root: حدث (ḥ-d-th)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Al-ḥudūth\": coming-to-be after non-existence; al-ḥadath: ritual impurity (as in this text's water analogy); al-muḥdath: the originated thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-ḥudūth: the thing's being after it was not — whether accident or substance. Its iḥdāth (bringing-about) is exclusive to God.\" Creaturely acts are originated, but their origination is divine.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: recent, newly arisen. Metaphorical: \"ʿUmar was muḥaddath\" (inspired in judgment) — as if spoken to by unseen truth; here, the \"newly arisen\" (ḥādith) effect is tied to a condition (sharṭ), not a new power.\n- Root: ماء (m-ā-ʾ)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Water as the origin of life ({وجعلنا من الماء كل شيء حي}); its root is m-w-h, with hāʾ dropped and wāw shifted — a paradigm for how a thing's \"presence\" (mulāqāh) does not entail its \"effect\" (taʾthīr) absent a condition.\n  * Kitab al-Ayn: Water's essence is unchanged; the analogy hinges on its constancy.\n- Root: وجه (w-j-h)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"Al-wajh\" is the face; then extended to the \"foremost\" or \"noblest\" part of any thing, and to the self (dhāt) in {ويبقى وجه ربك}. Here, \"washing the face\" is the specified condition (sharṭ) — the \"foremost\" ritual act — not the efficient cause of purification.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the bodily face. Metaphorical: \"wajh al-qawm\" (the leaders), \"wajh al-thawb\" (the surface of a garment) — the \"face\" as the operative locus; in the analogy, the face's washing is the trigger, not the agent.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Al-Faṣl bayna al-jār wa-al-majrūr (Prepositional Interposition) — Governing principle: a dependent element (here, divine power's \"attachment\" to objects) can be separated from its governor by an intervening condition, just as grammatical separation is permissible when the intervening clause is complete; analogically, the \"link\" (irṭibāṭ) of power to its effect is mediated by conditions without altering the agent's nature.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monotheism and Reliance'", "badge": "Insight"}], "19": [{"arabic": "علم القرآن {وقال} علمه البيان {وقال} ثم إن علينا بيانه {وقال} أفرأيتم ما تمنون أأنتم تخلقونه أم نحن الخالقون ثم قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم في وصف ملك الأرحام إنه يدخل الرحم فيأخذ النطفة في يده ثم يصورها جسدا فيقول يا رب أذكر أم أنثى أسوي أم معوج فيقول الله تعالى ما شاء ويخلق الملك (1) وفي لفظ آخر ويصور الملك ثم ينفخ فيه الروح بالسعادة أو بالشقاوة وقد قال بعض السلف إن الملك الذي يقال له الروح هو الذي يولج الأرواح في الأجساد وأنه يتنفس بوصفه فيكون كل نفس من أنفاسه روحا يلج في جسم ولذلك سمي روح", "english": "...the killing — rather, He stated explicitly, and He, exalted, said: {فَلَمْ تَقْتُلُوهُمْ وَلَٰكِنَّ اللَّهَ قَتَلَهُمْ} (\"You did not kill them, but God killed them\") and He, exalted, said: {وَمَا رَمَيْتَ إِذْ رَمَيْتَ وَلَٰكِنَّ اللَّهَ رَمَىٰ} (\"You did not throw when you threw, but God threw\"). This is a combination of negation and affirmation in appearance, but its meaning is: \"You did not throw\" in the sense by which the Lord is a thrower, \"when you threw\" in the sense by which the servant is a thrower — for the two are two distinct meanings.", "book_title": "Monitoring and Accounting", "section_title_ar": "Section 1115", "section_title_en": "On the Unification of Divine Agency and the Foundation of Trust (Tawḥīd as the Root of Tawakkul)", "section_index": 1115, "anchors": "- Root: ق-ت-ل (Q-T-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: \"to kill, to slay\" — the act of causing death by direct agency.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the Qur'anic context {فَلَمْ تَقْتُلُوهُمْ وَلَٰكِنَّ اللَّهَ قَتَلَهُمْ} (8:17), the verb is negated for the creature and affirmed for the Creator, indicating that true creative agency (ījād) belongs to God alone, while the creature's act is merely instrumental and attributive.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage assigns the verb to the true originator; metaphorical (majāz) usage assigns it to the proximate instrument, as in \"the king killed\" when the executioner strikes.\n- Root: ر-م-ي (R-M-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To throw, to cast\" — physical projection of an object.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In {وَمَا رَمَيْتَ إِذْ رَمَيْتَ وَلَٰكِنَّ اللَّهَ رَمَىٰ} (8:17), the dual affirmation and negation show two distinct meanings of \"throwing\": the human act (kasb) versus the divine act (khalq).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The literal sense belongs to the agent who originates the act ex nihilo; the metaphorical sense belongs to the one through whom the act occurs as a vessel.\n- Root: ع-ل-م (ʿ-L-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Knowledge, perception, cognition\" — the apprehension of a thing as it truly is.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Knowledge is of two kinds: (1) apprehension of the essence of a thing, and (2) judgment about a thing's existence or non-existence of attributes. Divine knowledge (ʿilm) is the source of all teaching, as in {عَلَّمَ بِالْقَلَمِ} (96:4).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"to know\" as direct cognition; also \"to teach\" (ʿallama) as the transmission of that cognition.\n- Root: و-ك-ل (W-K-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To entrust, to delegate, to rely upon\" — the act of appointing another as deputy and placing one's affair in their hands.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Tawakkul is to rely upon another and make them one's agent. The Wakīl (Trustee) is He who undertakes the affair of His servant, as in {وَكَفَىٰ بِاللَّهِ وَكِيلًا} (4:81).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"to entrust an affair to someone.\" Metaphorical: \"to rely upon\" in the sense of inner confidence and tranquility of heart.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle \"innamā\" restricts the predicate to the subject alone, excluding all others. Also applicable: the rule of negation and affirmation (nafy wa-ithbāt) in a single sentence, where the negation applies to one sense and the affirmation to another, requiring semantic disambiguation (taṣrīf al-maʿnā).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monitoring and Accounting'", "badge": "Insight"}], "20": [{"arabic": "وقال عز وجل كلا بل تحبون العاجلة وتذرون الآخرة فمن هذا حده فينبغي أن يعالج قلبه من حب الجاه بالعلم بالآفات العاجلة وهو أن يتفكر في الأخطار التي يستهدف لها أرباب الجاه في الدنيا فإن كل ذي جاه محسود ومقصود بالإيذاء وخائف على الدوام على جاهه ومحترز من أن تتغير منزلته في القلوب والقلوب أشد تغيرا من القدر في غليانها وهي مترددة بين الإقبال والإعراض فكل ما يبنى على قلوب الخلق يضاهي ما يبنى على أمواج البحر فإنه لا ثبات له والاشتغال بمراعاة القلوب وحفظ الجاه ودفع كيد الحساد ومنع أذى الأعداء كل ذلك غ", "english": "And it is as though you had never ceased to be in the Hereafter — for these people, their attention was turned to the final outcome, and so their deeds were performed for its sake, through godfearingness (taqwā), since they knew that the final outcome belongs to the godfearing. Thus they held in contempt rank and wealth in this world. But the sight of most creatures is weak, confined to the immediate (al-ʿājilah), whose light does not extend to the witnessing of final outcomes. For this reason, Allah Most High said: {«بل تؤثرون الحياة الدنيا والآخرة خير وأبقى»} (\"Nay, you prefer the life of this world, while the Hereafter is better and more enduring\") [Al-Aʿlā 87:16–17]. And He, Mighty and Majestic, said: {«كلا بل تحبون العاجلة وتذرون الآخرة»} (\"Nay, but you love the immediate and forsake the Hereafter\") [Al-Qiyāmah 75:20–21].", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 804", "section_title_en": "The Cure for Love of Status — Section 804 of The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 804, "anchors": "- Root: جاه (jāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الجاه: القدر والمنزلة، وجاه عند الناس: وقع وعظم\" — rank, standing, and elevation in the eyes of others.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: jāh is a social accident (ʿaraḍ) attached to the estimations of hearts, not a real attribute of the self; its pursuit is a veiling distraction from the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"جاه عند السلطان: منزلته ووجهته.\" Metaphorical (majāz): the \"face\" (wajh) one presents to people — i.e., honor is but a reflected image in fluctuating hearts, like a building upon waves.\n- Root: خطر (khaṭar)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الخطر: الإشراف على الهلاك، والخطر: السبق الذي يراهن عليه\" — peril and stakes.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The \"danger\" (khaṭar) of status is that its possessor becomes a target (gharaḍ) for envy and harm — a Quranic motif of the world's transient allure versus the abiding Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"خاطر بنفسه: ألقاها في المهلكة.\" Metaphorical: the aspirant to jāh \"stakes\" his own peace as wager in a game he cannot win.\n- Root: خمل (khamūl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الخمول: خفاء الذكر وعدم الشهرة، وخمل الرجل: سقط ولم يُعرف\" — obscurity and anonymity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Obscurity (khamūl) is the therapeutic counterweight to jāh; it severs the heart's attachment to created estimations.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"خمل الثوب: ما خفي من نسجه.\" Metaphorical: the soul's \"hidden weave\" — its true worth lies unseen, like the inner thread of cloth.\n- Root: قنع (qanāʿah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"قنع: رضي بالكفاف واكتفى باليسير\" — contentment with sufficiency.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Qanāʿah is the root that severs ṭamaʿ (covetous expectation) from creatures, redirecting hope solely to the Creator.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قنع الصائد: نصب القنوع\" (the hunter's blind). Metaphorical: the contented man \"blinds\" himself to people's opinions, as one hides in a blind to escape prey's notice.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the contrastive structure \"إنما يعالج بالعلم... وأما من حيث العمل\" where the particle ḥaṣr restricts each cure to its proper domain (knowledge vs. practice), and \"فإن\" clauses provide causal grounding for periodic sentences.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}], "22": [{"arabic": "لقوله تعالى وجوه يومئذ ناضرة إلى ربها ناظرة ولا يرى في الدنيا تصديقا لقوله عز وجل لا تدركه الأبصار وهو يدرك الأبصار ولقوله تعالى في خطاب موسى عليه السلام لن تراني وليت شعري كيف عرف المعتزل من صفات رب الأرباب ما جهله موسى عليه السلام وكيف سأل موسى عليه السلام الرؤية مع كونها محالا ولعل الجهل بذوي البدع والأهواء من الجهلة الأغبياء أولى من الجهل بالأنبياء صلوات الله عليهم وأما وجه إجراء آية الرؤية على الظاهر فهو أنه غير مؤد إلى المحال فإن الرؤية نوع كشف وعلم إلا أنه أتم وأوضح من العلم فإذا جا", "english": "**The Eighth Principle: Knowledge that He, Exalted is He, is established upon His Throne (mustawin ʿalā ʿarshihi) in the sense that God, Exalted is He, intended by istiwāʾ — which is the sense that does not contradict the attribute of Majesty, nor does any mark of origination (ḥudūth) or passing away (fanāʾ) attach to it. And it is the sense intended by His saying — istiwāʾ toward the heaven — where He said in the Qur'an: {«ثُمَّ اسْتَوَىٰ إِلَى السَّمَاءِ وَهِيَ دُخَانٌ»} (\"Then He directed Himself to the heaven, and it was smoke\") [Fussilat 41:11]. And that is only by way of subjugation (qahr) and dominion (istīlāʾ), as the poet said:", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 90", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 90: The Ten Principles Concerning Knowledge of the Divine Attributes", "section_index": 90, "anchors": "- Root: ع ل م (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denoting the faculty of perception and comprehension; the mark or trace by which something is known.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of the essence and judgment upon it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Distinguishes literal knowledge (ḥaqīqah) from awareness (shiʿr); here, it denotes certain, verified cognition of theological verities.\n- Root: س و ي (s-w-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root indicating straightness, equality, and levelness; from it derives istiwāʾ (establishing oneself upon, ascending to).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Istiwāʾ upon the Throne (al-ʿArsh) is a divine act of sovereign dominion, not corporeal settlement.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal sense of physical sitting is impossible for God; metaphorical sense (majāz) of qahr (overpowering) and istīlāʾ (dominion) is necessitated by reason and consensus.\n- Root: ر أ ي (r-ʾ-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Visual perception; also opinion and discernment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ruʾyah (vision) of God in the Hereafter is a mode of disclosure (kashf) and knowledge (ʿilm), more complete than intellectual cognition, not conditioned by spatial direction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vision requires corporeality and direction; its application to God demands theological specification (takhṣīṣ) without nullifying the veracity of the scriptural promise.\n- Root: ق د ر (q-d-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Power, measure, decree; the capacity to bring about an act.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Divine power (qudrah) is the negation of incapacity (ʿajz); absolute power belongs to God alone, while creatures possess limited capacity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal power over an object; metaphorically, God's power encompasses all things without exertion or instrument.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Prepositional Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — governing the permissible separation between the preposition and its object for rhetorical emphasis, applied here to the parsing of \"إلى السماء\" in the verse on istiwāʾ, where the prepositional phrase is delayed to highlight the divine act of sovereign direction toward the heavens.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}], "33": [{"arabic": "وفي معناه قول العرب تغد تمد تعش تمش يعني تمدد كما قال الله تعال ثم ذهب إلى أهله يتمطى أي يتمطط ويقال إن حبس البول يفسد الجسد كما يفسد النهر ما حوله إذا سد مجراه الرابع في الخبر قطع العروق مسقمة وترك العشاء مهرمة (2) والعرب تقول ترك الغداء يذهب بشحم الكاذة يعني الآلية وقال بعض الحكماء لابنه يا بني لا تخرج من منزلك حتى تأخذ حلمك أي تتغذى إذ به يبقى الحلم ويزول الطيش وهو أيضا أقل لشهوته لما يرى في السوق وقال حكيم لسمين أرى عليك قطيفة من نسج أضراسك فمم هي قال من أكل لباب البر وصغار المعز وأدهن بجام بنفسج وألبس الكتان الخامس الحمية تضر", "english": "**The First [Matter]:** It is related of Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī that he said: \"Eating in the marketplace is baseness.\" He attributed this to the Messenger of God (peace be upon him), and its chain of transmission is sound and close. Yet the opposite has been transmitted from Ibn ʿUmar (may God be pleased with them both), who said: \"We used to eat during the era of the Messenger of God (peace be upon him) while walking, and we would drink while standing.\" And some of the well-known shaykhs of the Sufis was seen eating in the marketplace, and it was said to him regarding that. He replied: \"Woe to you! I become hungry in the marketplace, so shall I eat in the house?\" It was said: \"Then do you enter the mosque?\" He said: \"I am too ashamed to enter His house merely to eat therein.\"", "book_title": "Manners of Eating", "section_title_ar": "Section 292", "section_title_en": "On the Etiquette of Eating: The First Discourse on the Manners of Nourishment", "section_index": 292, "anchors": "- Root: اكل (akl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is the physical act of consuming food; also used for the action of fire upon fuel, and for usury (ribā) in juridical metaphor.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Distinguishes al-akl (the act of eating) from al-ukl (that which is eaten, sustenance). The term carries a metaphorical extension to any form of consumption or acquisition.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah) for mastication and swallowing; metaphorical (majāz) for consuming wealth unjustly, for the action of fire, and for the \"eating\" of one's reputation through slander.\n- Root: رسل (rasl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To send forth gently, to dispatch with ease; a messenger (rasūl) is one sent with a message.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The root implies gentle, measured dispatch; from it derives the concept of the divine message and the angelic emissary.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for sending a person or animal; metaphorical for the sending of divine chastisement or the \"messengers of death\" (arrows).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — Governing how general statements about eating are restricted by the specific circumstances of the person, place, and intention, as exemplified in the first point's reconciliation of contradictory reports.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Manners of Eating'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "36": [{"arabic": "نطفة فإذا هو خصيم مبين} وقال تعالى {أيحسب الإنسان أن يترك سدى ألم يك نطفة من مني يمنى ثم كان علقة فخلق فسوى فجعل منه الزوجين الذكر والأنثى} ففى خلق الآدمى مع كثرة عجائبه واختلاف تركيب أعضائه أعاجيب تزيد على الأعاجيب فى بعثه وإعادته فكيف ينكر ذلك من قدرة الله تعالى وحكمته من يشاهد ذلك فى صنعته وقدرته فإن كان فى إيمانك ضعف فقو الإيمان بالنظر فى النشأة الأولى فإن الثانية مثلها وأسهل منها وإن كنت قوى الإيمان بها فأشعر قلبك تلك المخاوف والأخطار وأكثر فيها التفكر والاعتبار لتسلب عن قلبك الراحة والقرار فتشتغل بالتشمر للعرض على الجبار وتفكر أولا فيما يقرع سمع سكان القبور من شدة ن", "english": "The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Allah the Exalted said: \"The son of Adam has reviled Me, and he had no right to revile Me; and he has denied Me, and he had no right to deny Me. As for his reviling of Me, it is his saying that I have a son. And as for his denial of Me, it is his saying: 'He will never bring me back as He created me at first.'\" {«شتمني ابن آدم وما ينبغي له أن يشتمني وكذبني وما ينبغي له أن يكذبني...»}", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1335", "section_title_en": "On the Terrors of the Trumpet-Blast and the Awakening for the Resurrection", "section_index": 1335, "anchors": "- Root: نفخ (N-F-Kh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To blow into a thing, to inflate; the breath of wind entering a vessel or body.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The divine act of breathing spirit into creation {«ونفخت فيه من روحي»}; here, the eschatological blast that annihilates and then revives all beings.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage of blowing into the Trumpet (الصور); metaphorical extension to the \"blast of spring\" reviving the earth—here applied to the two cosmic blasts of death and resurrection.\n- Root: صور (S-W-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Form, image, shape; also a horn or trumpet (the instrument of the blast).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The perceptible form of bodies and the intelligible form of souls; here, the Trumpet (الصور) as the instrument of divine command that re-forms and re-shapes creation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the horn (القرن) held by Israfil; metaphorical: the \"inclination\" (صورة) of hearts toward terror and anticipation.\n- Root: موت (M-W-T)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Death as the cessation of life; the state of the corpse.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Death as the separation of the soul from the body; the \"first death\" of the blast and the \"second death\" of the wicked.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical death; metaphorical: the \"death\" of the heart through heedlessness, and the reviving of dead lands by rain—here, the reviving of bodies by the second blast.\n- Root: قبر (Q-B-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The grave as the abode of the dead; burial.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The grave as the intermediate state (barzakh) before resurrection; {«حتى زرتم المقابر»} as a metaphor for death.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the grave and its inhabitants; metaphorical: the \"raising of the nose\" (قبراه) of the proud—contrasted here with the humility of the dead rising from their graves.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to emphasize that true faith is not mere verbal assent but heartfelt conviction, as in \"إنما فتور البواطن... لقلة الفهم\" (The languor of the inner selves is only due to the lack of understanding).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}], "37": [{"arabic": "وعرف خسته أولا فقال {ألم يك نطفة من مني يمنى ثم كان علقة} ثم ذكر منته عليه فقال {فخلق فسوى فجعل منه الزوجين الذكر والأنثى} ليدوم وجوده بالتناسل كما حصل وجوده أولا بالاختراع فمن كان هذا بدؤه وهذه أحواله فمن أين له البطر والكبرياء والفخر والخيلاء وهو على التحقيق أخس الأخساء وأضعف الضعفاء ولكن هذه عادة الخسيس إذا رفع من خسته شمخ بأنفه وتعظم وذلك لدلالة خسة أوله ولا حول ولا قوة إلا بالله نعم لو أكمله وفوض إليه أمره وأدام له الوجود باختياره لجاز أن يطغى وينسى المبدأ والمنتهى ولكنه سلط عليه في", "english": "As for his end and his final destination — it is death, indicated by the saying of God Most High: {ثم أماته فأقبره ثم إذا شاء أنشره} — \"Then He caused him to die and buried him; then, when He wills, He shall raise him.\" Its meaning is that He strips from him his spirit, his hearing, his sight, his knowledge, his power, his sense, his perception, and his movement — so he returns to being an inert thing (jamād), just as he was the first time, with nothing remaining except the shape of his limbs and his form — no sense in him, no movement. Then he is placed in the earth, and becomes a putrid, filthy carcass, just as he was at first a contemptible drop. Then his limbs decay, his parts crumble, his bones rot, and he becomes dust and fragments; the worm eats his parts — beginning with his two pupils, plucking them out; then his cheeks, cutting them away; then the rest of his parts — until he becomes dung in the bellies of worms, a carcass from which the animal flees, which every man deems filthy and shuns for the intensity of its stench. And the best of his states is that he returns to what he was: he becomes earth, from which jugs are made and from which buildings are made. So he becomes nonexistent after having been existent, and becomes as though he had never flourished yesterday — reaped down — just as he was in his first affair for a long span. And would that he had remained so — how good it would be if he were left as earth!", "book_title": "Monitoring and Accounting", "section_title_ar": "Section 864", "section_title_en": "On the Baseness of Man's Origin and the Folly of Pride — Section 864 of The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 864, "anchors": "- Root: ض ل ل (ḍ-l-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core sense of going astray, being lost, or perishing; also used for being concealed or obliterated.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological context, denotes deviation from the straight path (al-ṣirāṭ al-mustaqīm) and the state of being bereft of divine guidance — here, the primordial state of non-existence is framed as utter loss.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) for physical straying; metaphorical (majāz) for spiritual ruin — applied here to the pre-creation void.\n- Root: ن ط ف (n-ṭ-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Water that flows or drips; a drop; specifically the seminal fluid.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النطفة: الماء الصافي، ويعبر بها عن ماء الرجل\" — pure water, used as a euphemism for male semen; also the pearl (al-luʾluʾah) in its purity, but here contrasted with filth (qadhir).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for clear water; metaphorical for the base origin of man — the drop is both physically and morally negligible.\n- Root: م ل ك (m-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Possession, dominion, and control; the hand that grasps and holds.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ownership and sovereign power; in Kalam, distinguishes the Creator's absolute dominion (mulk) from the creature's borrowed, contingent capacity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for legal possession; metaphorical for self-mastery — man \"does not possess his own heart\" (lā yamliku qalbahu), a rhetorical denial of autonomous agency.\n- Root: ك ب ر (k-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Greatness in size, rank, or dignity; also arrogance (kibr) as its blameworthy derivative.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Grandeur (kibriyāʾ) is exclusively divine; human pride is a usurpation of an attribute that belongs to God alone.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical magnitude; metaphorical for moral haughtiness — the text pivots on this tension.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The repeated use of \"إنما\" (only, solely) in the opening lines restricts the cause of man's creation and blessing to a single purpose: self-knowledge and knowledge of God, not self-glorification.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Monitoring and Accounting'", "badge": "Ethics"}, {"arabic": "ألم يك نطفة من مني يمنى ثم كان علقة فخلق فسوى وقال تعالى ألم نخلقكم من ماء مهين فجعلناه في قرار مكين إلى قدر معلوم {وقال} أولم ير الإنسان أنا خلقناه من نطفة فإذا هو خصيم مبين {وقال} إنا خلقنا الإنسان من نطفة أمشاج ثم ذكر كيف جعل النطفة علقة والعلقة مضغة والمضغة عظاما فقال تعالى ولقد خلقنا الإنسان من سلالة من طين ثم جعلناه نطفة في قرار مكين ثم خلقنا النطفة علقة الآية فتكرير ذكر النطفة في الكتاب العزيز ليس ليسمع لفظه ويترك التفكر في معناه فانظر الآن إلى النطفة وهي قطرة من الماء قذرة لو تركت سا", "english": "I say: The Exalted said: {أَلَمْ يَكُ نُطْفَةً مِنْ مَنِيٍّ يُمْنَىٰ ثُمَّ كَانَ عَلَقَةً فَخَلَقَ فَسَوَّىٰ} [Was he not a sperm-drop from semen emitted? Then he was a clinging clot, and He created and proportioned him] (Al-Qiyamah: 37-38). And the Exalted said: {أَلَمْ نَخْلُقْكُمْ مِنْ مَاءٍ مَهِينٍ فَجَعَلْنَاهُ فِي قَرَارٍ مَكِينٍ إِلَىٰ قَدَرٍ مَعْلُومٍ} [Did We not create you from a despicable fluid, then We placed it in a firm resting-place for a known term?] (Al-Mursalat: 20-22). And He said: {أَوَلَمْ يَرَ الْإِنْسَانُ أَنَّا خَلَقْنَاهُ مِنْ نُطْفَةٍ فَإِذَا هُوَ خَصِيمٌ مُبِينٌ} [Does man not see that We created him from a sperm-drop, yet behold, he is a manifest adversary?] (Ya-Sin: 77). And He said: {إِنَّا خَلَقْنَا الْإِنْسَانَ مِنْ نُطْفَةٍ أَمْشَاجٍ} [Indeed, We created man from a mixed sperm-drop] (Al-Insan: 2). Then He mentioned how He made the sperm-drop a clinging clot, the clot a chewed lump, and the lump bones, saying: {وَلَقَدْ خَلَقْنَا الْإِنْسَانَ مِنْ سُلَالَةٍ مِنْ طِينٍ ثُمَّ جَعَلْنَاهُ نُطْفَةً فِي قَرَارٍ مَكِينٍ ثُمَّ خَلَقْنَا النُّطْفَةَ عَلَقَةً} [And indeed We created man from an extract of clay, then We made him a sperm-drop in a firm resting-place, then We created the sperm-drop into a clinging clot] (Al-Mu'minun: 12-14), to the end of the verse.", "book_title": "Contemplation", "section_title_ar": "Section 1269", "section_title_en": "The Wonders of Human Creation from a Sperm-Drop — Section 1269", "section_index": 1269, "anchors": "- Root: نطف (n-ṭ-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Pure, clear water; a drop of fluid; the seminal fluid; also pearl.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النطفة: الماء الصافي\" — the sperm-drop as pure water, used Quranically for the seminal fluid; a technical term for the initial stage of human embryological creation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"الماء الصافي قل أو كثر\" — clear water, little or much. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ليلة نطوف: مطرت حتى الصباح\" — a night of continuous rain, extending the root's sense of flowing/pouring.\n- Root: خلق (kh-l-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Measuring, estimating, fashioning precisely; creating from nothing or from a prior substance.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الخلق أصله: التقدير المستقيم\" — origination upon a straight measure; used for both creation ex nihilo (إبداع) and formation from an existing thing (إيجاد من الشيء).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قدره قبل القطع\" — measuring before cutting (craftsman's act). Metaphorical: \"خلق الله الخلق: أوجده على تقدير أوجبته الحكمة\" — God's creation as bringing into existence upon a measure necessitated by wisdom.\n- Root: كيف (kayf)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: An interrogative particle asking about state or condition; also used for manner.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"كيف: لفظ يسأل به عما يصح أن يقال فيه: شبيه وغير شبيه\" — a word asking about what admits of likeness or unlikeness (qualities); not applicable to God's essence, but used here to provoke reflection on creaturely processes.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Interrogative of modality; in this context, a rhetorical summons to contemplate the \"how\" of divine craftsmanship.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The periodic structure here repeatedly employs \"ثم كيف\" (then how) as a coordinating interrogative chain, linking successive clauses without ellipsis, preserving the full syntactic weight of each stage of creation.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Contemplation'", "badge": "Insight"}], "39": [{"arabic": "عليه فقال {فخلق فسوى فجعل منه الزوجين الذكر والأنثى} ليدوم وجوده بالتناسل كما حصل وجوده أولا بالاختراع فمن كان هذا بدؤه وهذه أحواله فمن أين له البطر والكبرياء والفخر والخيلاء وهو على التحقيق أخس الأخساء وأضعف الضعفاء ولكن هذه عادة الخسيس إذا رفع من خسته شمخ بأنفه وتعظم وذلك لدلالة خسة أوله ولا حول ولا قوة إلا بالله نعم لو أكمله وفوض إليه أمره وأدام له الوجود باختياره لجاز أن يطغى وينسى المبدأ والمنتهى ولكنه سلط عليه في دوام وجوده الأمراض الهائلة والأسقام العظيمة والآفات المختلفة والطباع المتضادة من", "english": "As for his end and his final destination — it is death, indicated by the saying of God Most High: {ثم أماته فأقبره ثم إذا شاء أنشره} — \"Then He caused him to die and buried him; then, when He wills, He shall raise him.\" Its meaning is that He strips from him his spirit, his hearing, his sight, his knowledge, his power, his sense, his perception, and his movement — so he returns to being an inert thing (jamād), just as he was the first time, with nothing remaining except the shape of his limbs and his form — no sense in him, no movement. Then he is placed in the earth, and becomes a putrid, filthy carcass, just as he was at first a contemptible drop. Then his limbs decay, his parts crumble, his bones rot, and he becomes dust and fragments; the worm eats his parts — beginning with his two pupils, plucking them out; then his cheeks, cutting them away; then the rest of his parts — until he becomes dung in the bellies of worms, a carcass from which the animal flees, which every man deems filthy and shuns for the intensity of its stench. And the best of his states is that he returns to what he was: he becomes earth, from which jugs are made and from which buildings are made. So he becomes nonexistent after having been existent, and becomes as though he had never flourished yesterday — reaped down — just as he was in his first affair for a long span. And would that he had remained so — how good it would be if he were left as earth!", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 864", "section_title_en": "On the Baseness of Man's Origin and the Folly of Pride — Section 864 of The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 864, "anchors": "- Root: ض ل ل (ḍ-l-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core sense of going astray, being lost, or perishing; also used for being concealed or obliterated.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological context, denotes deviation from the straight path (al-ṣirāṭ al-mustaqīm) and the state of being bereft of divine guidance — here, the primordial state of non-existence is framed as utter loss.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) for physical straying; metaphorical (majāz) for spiritual ruin — applied here to the pre-creation void.\n- Root: ن ط ف (n-ṭ-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Water that flows or drips; a drop; specifically the seminal fluid.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النطفة: الماء الصافي، ويعبر بها عن ماء الرجل\" — pure water, used as a euphemism for male semen; also the pearl (al-luʾluʾah) in its purity, but here contrasted with filth (qadhir).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for clear water; metaphorical for the base origin of man — the drop is both physically and morally negligible.\n- Root: م ل ك (m-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Possession, dominion, and control; the hand that grasps and holds.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ownership and sovereign power; in Kalam, distinguishes the Creator's absolute dominion (mulk) from the creature's borrowed, contingent capacity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for legal possession; metaphorical for self-mastery — man \"does not possess his own heart\" (lā yamliku qalbahu), a rhetorical denial of autonomous agency.\n- Root: ك ب ر (k-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Greatness in size, rank, or dignity; also arrogance (kibr) as its blameworthy derivative.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Grandeur (kibriyāʾ) is exclusively divine; human pride is a usurpation of an attribute that belongs to God alone.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical magnitude; metaphorical for moral haughtiness — the text pivots on this tension.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The repeated use of \"إنما\" (only, solely) in the opening lines restricts the cause of man's creation and blessing to a single purpose: self-knowledge and knowledge of God, not self-glorification.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "40": [{"arabic": "تكونى نطفة من مني يمنى ثم كنت علقة فخلق فسوى أليس ذلك بقادر على أن يحيى الموتى فإن كان هذا من إضمارك فما أكفرك وأجهلك أما تتفكرين أنه مماذا خلقك من نطفة خلقك فقدرك ثم السبيل يسرك ثم أماتك فأقبرك أفتكذبينه في قوله ثم إذا شاء أنشرك فإن لم تكونى مكذبة فما لك لا تأخذين حذرك ولو أن يهوديا أخبرك في ألذ أطعمتك بأنه يضرك في مرضك لصبرت عنه وتركته وجاهدت نفسك فيه أفكان قول الأنبياء المؤيدين بالمعجزات وقول الله تعالى في كتبه المنزلة أقل عندك تأثيرا من قول يهودى يخبرك عن حدس وتخمين وظن مع نقصان عقل وقصور علم والعجب أنه لو أخبرك طفل ب", "english": "...and intelligence and acumen, while you are the most stupid and foolish of people? Do you not recognize what lies before you of the Garden and the Fire, and that you are hastening to one of the two imminently? So why do you rejoice and laugh and occupy yourself with frivolity while you are being sought for this tremendous calamity? Perhaps today you will be snatched away, or tomorrow. I see that you regard death as distant, but God regards it as near. Do you not know that everything that is coming is near, and that the distant is only that which is not coming? Do you not know that death comes suddenly, without a prior messenger, without prior appointment or covenant? It does not come in one thing rather than another, nor in winter rather than summer, nor in summer rather than winter, nor in day rather than night, nor in night rather than day. It does not come in youth rather than old age, nor in old age rather than youth. Rather, every single breath could be the moment of sudden death. And if death does not come suddenly, then illness comes suddenly, and then leads to death. So why do you not prepare for death, when it is nearer to you than anything near? Do you not ponder the saying of God Most High: {«اقترب للناس حسابهم وهم في غفلة معرضون * ما يأتيهم من ذكر من ربهم محدث إلا استمعوه وهم يلعبون * لاهية قلوبهم»} (The reckoning for mankind has drawn near, while they turn away in heedlessness. No fresh reminder comes to them from their Lord but they listen to it while they play—their hearts distracted). Woe to you, O soul! If your audacity toward disobeying God stems from your belief that God does not see you, then how immense is your disbelief! And if it is despite your knowledge of His awareness of you, then how severe is your shamelessness and how little your modesty! Woe to you, O soul! If a slave of your slaves—nay, a brother among your brothers—were to confront you with what you detest, how would your anger and hatred toward him be? So with what audacity do you expose yourself to God's hatred, His wrath, and His severe punishment? Do you think you can endure His chastisement? Far be it, far be it! Test yourself—if heedless pride has distracted you from His painful punishment, then restrain yourself for an hour in the sun, or in the bathhouse, or bring your finger near the fire, so that your capacity may become clear to you. Are you deluded by God's generosity, His bounty, and His independence from your obedience and worship? Then why do you not rely on God's generosity in the important matters of your worldly life? When an enemy approaches you, why do you devise stratagems to repel him, rather than entrusting him to God's generosity? And when you are pressed by a need for one of the worldly desires that cannot be fulfilled except through dinar and dirham, why do you strain your very soul in seeking and procuring it through various contrivances, without relying on God's generosity—so that He might lead you to a treasure, or subjugate one of His slaves to carry your need to you without any effort or seeking on your part? Do you suppose that God is generous in the Hereafter but not in this world, when you already know that the sunnah of God admits no change, and that the Lord of the Hereafter and this world is One, and that {«ليس للإنسان إلا ما سعى»} (man shall have nothing but what he strives for)?", "book_title": "Monitoring and Accounting", "section_title_ar": "Section 1251", "section_title_en": "Section 1251 — The Admonition of the Soul Concerning the Nearness of Death and the Duplicity of False Hopes", "section_index": 1251, "anchors": "- Root: قرب (q-r-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Proximity in place, time, or rank; nearness of relation or degree.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Used for spatial, temporal, and relational nearness; here, divine nearness of death and the Hour—a metaphysical proximity, not measurable distance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) for physical closeness; metaphorical (majāz) for what is inevitable and imminent—\"كل ما هو آت قريب\" (all that is coming is near).\n- Root: موت (m-w-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Death as the cessation of animal life; also used metaphorically for lifelessness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Death as transition, not annihilation; linked to resurrection (baʿth) and divine power.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for bodily death; metaphorical for the \"death\" of heedlessness or the dead heart.\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that which desires and is accountable.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The soul as the locus of blameworthy traits (al-nafs al-ammārah) versus the rational, discerning faculty; here, self-address in reproach.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for the animate self; metaphorical for the inner voice of conscience rebuking the lower soul.\n- Root: طلب (ṭ-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Seeking, pursuing, demanding.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The active search for something, whether physical or conceptual; here, the soul's frantic pursuit of worldly desires versus its neglect of what is truly sought from it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for seeking provisions; metaphorical for the soul being \"sought\" by death—\"وأنت مطلوبة\" (you are being sought).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rhetorical separation between the preposition and its object to emphasize urgency, as in \"على القرب\" and \"أقرب إليك من كل قريب,\" where proximity is stressed through syntactic placement.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monitoring and Accounting'", "badge": "Insight"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 530, 2401], [2, 2401, 3400], [3, 3400, 4634], [4, 4634, 6120]], "75:1": [[1, 530, 2401], [2, 2401, 3400], [3, 3400, 4634], [4, 4634, 6120]], "2": [[1, 20, 2283], [2, 2283, 3239], [3, 3239, 4548], [4, 4548, 6870]], "75:2": [[1, 20, 2283], [2, 2283, 3239], [3, 3239, 4548], [4, 4548, 6870]], "3": [[1, 10, 1676], [2, 1676, 3275], [3, 3275, 4243], [4, 4243, 5651], [5, 5651, 7390]], "75:3": [[1, 10, 1676], [2, 1676, 3275], [3, 3275, 4243], [4, 4243, 5651], [5, 5651, 7390]], "4": [[1, 420, 1367], [2, 1367, 2834], [3, 2834, 4667], [4, 4667, 5094], [5, 5094, 6860], [6, 6860, 8140]], "75:4": [[1, 420, 1367], [2, 1367, 2834], [3, 2834, 4667], [4, 4667, 5094], [5, 5094, 6860], [6, 6860, 8140]], "5": [[1, 10, 474], [2, 474, 2052], [3, 2052, 3567], [4, 3567, 5052], [5, 5052, 6630]], "75:5": [[1, 10, 474], [2, 474, 2052], [3, 2052, 3567], [4, 3567, 5052], [5, 5052, 6630]], "6": [[1, 20, 963], [2, 963, 2788], [3, 2788, 3791], [4, 3791, 5240]], "75:6": [[1, 20, 963], [2, 963, 2788], [3, 2788, 3791], [4, 3791, 5240]], "7": [[1, 30, 1452], [2, 1